WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.502
Director:
All right, check the gate, guys.

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:03.712
I'll go on three?

00:00:03.712 --> 00:00:05.631
Director:
Three, two, one, action!

00:00:05.631 --> 00:00:14.348
♪♪

00:00:14.348 --> 00:00:17.518
David S. Goyer:
Foundation is the greatest
science fiction work of all time.

00:00:17.518 --> 00:00:17.935
♪♪

00:00:17.935 --> 00:00:21.063
It's a story of ideas and they
are bold ideas.

00:00:21.063 --> 00:00:23.232
♪♪

00:00:23.232 --> 00:00:25.817
David S. Goyer:
The visual effects are really
cutting edge.

00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:29.196
David S. Goyer:
We're going to see things that
we haven't ever seen in a series before.

00:00:29.196 --> 00:00:32.908
♪♪

00:00:32.908 --> 00:00:35.160
Chris MacLean:
It was a huge collaborative
effort.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.247
Chris MacLean:
It was a thousand people working
on the show to get it finished,

00:00:38.247 --> 00:00:40.415
just from a visual effects
perspective.

00:00:41.416 --> 00:00:43.669
(Ship engines noise)

00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:46.255
Rory Cheyne:
We had so many different minds
working on this project.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:51.885
It was a real global effort to
get it designed and built and
shot, for sure.

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.845
♪♪

00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:55.597
Ian Hunter:
These days it's mostly digitally
done.

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:58.600
Ian Hunter:
But, in the case of foundation,
we're actually going to

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.395
Ian Hunter:
build miniature models of 
some of the spacecrafts

00:01:01.395 --> 00:01:02.396
Ian Hunter:
and some of the locations

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.648
and then integrate them into the
visual effects.

00:01:04.648 --> 00:01:06.483
♪♪

00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:08.026
Ian Hunter:
We start with concept art,

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.903
turning it into a digital model.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:13.532
Ian Hunter:
We can then translate that
digital model into a file

00:01:13.532 --> 00:01:15.033
Ian Hunter:
that we can use to 3-D print.

00:01:15.617 --> 00:01:19.079
So we're taking the digital
asset and turning it into a
physical asset.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:20.706
♪♪ 

00:01:20.706 --> 00:01:21.915
Ian Hunter:
We're shooting things physically

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:23.917
Ian Hunter:
like, blowing things up in the
cloud tanks 

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:26.795
Ian Hunter:
or shooting our miniatures
against an LED screen.

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:28.088
♪♪

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:30.173
Ian Hunter:
Photographing it with a motion
control cameras.

00:01:30.716 --> 00:01:34.303
Ian Hunter:
We're taking different methods
of doing visuals, 

00:01:34.303 --> 00:01:36.847
trying to get every physical
aspect we can.

00:01:36.847 --> 00:01:46.315
♪♪

00:01:46.315 --> 00:01:48.567
Bart:
Whenever we approach science
fiction, 

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:50.152
we always want it to feel
grounded.

00:01:50.152 --> 00:01:53.238
Like exploring the look and
style of the costumes.

00:01:53.238 --> 00:01:56.950
Bart:
We're looking at like desert
societies and Inuit societies.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:57.784
♪♪

00:01:57.784 --> 00:01:59.661
Kurt:
You have these different
characters coming from different

00:01:59.661 --> 00:02:02.289
Kurt:
worlds, it's easier to look at
those characters and think,

00:02:02.289 --> 00:02:03.498
Kurt:
"where are they coming from?"

00:02:03.498 --> 00:02:05.792
Like Gaal came from Synnax,
which is a water planet. 

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:06.752
♪♪

00:02:06.752 --> 00:02:09.421
Bart:
Each world, we took what their
environment was,

00:02:09.421 --> 00:02:11.590
for design choices to help make 

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:14.634
our Foundation universe look
completely original.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:17.846
♪♪

00:02:17.846 --> 00:02:19.848
Alfred Enoch:
You have characters coming from
all sorts of 

00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:22.059
different worlds, different
classes.

00:02:22.059 --> 00:02:25.020
Alfred Enoch:
They'll dress different and
they'll sound differently.

00:02:28.565 --> 00:02:29.441
Phara:
(Speaking Anacreon)

00:02:29.441 --> 00:02:32.152
Fionnuala Murphy:
Most languages have different
patterns.

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:34.946
Something that would make them unique,
stand out.

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:36.782
♪♪

00:02:36.782 --> 00:02:39.326
Fionnuala Murphy:
People from Anacreon, these are
warrior like,

00:02:39.326 --> 00:02:41.161
Fionnuala Murphy:
So they're blunt, they're hard
headed

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.287
Fionnuala Murphy:
They just come out with it.

00:02:42.287 --> 00:02:43.872
(Speaking Anacreon)

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:47.000
Fionnuala Murphy:
The other language Thespis,
they're a bit more laid back. 

00:02:47.334 --> 00:02:49.628
Fionnuala Murphy:
So that gives certain qualities
to the languages.

00:02:49.628 --> 00:02:50.462
That sets them apart.

00:02:50.462 --> 00:02:53.090
♪♪

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.176
David Kob:
With Foundation, we're trying to
do for science fiction 

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:57.594
David Kob:
what Game Of Thrones did for
fantasy.

00:02:58.178 --> 00:03:00.180
David Kob:
Which is present a fully
realized world

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.266
with a level of sophistication
that's never been seen on
television before.

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:06.645
Lou Llobell:
Seeing the work that everyone
puts in

00:03:06.645 --> 00:03:09.439
Lou Llobell:
and how much love has gone into
it,

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:11.024
it's going to be one heck of
a show.

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:20.409
♪♪

